{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>What the worker can't do alone, and how to let people cancel</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A worker has no DOM access, it can't create the <code>&lt;a download&gt;</code> element and click it. The download trigger always has to happen back on the main thread, off the worker's final message, the worker's job ends at handing back the finished data.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Give the export a real cancel button, not just a visual "loading" state. <code>worker.terminate()</code> stops the worker immediately, mid-loop, something a synchronous main-thread export can never offer since there's no opportunity for anything else to run, including a cancel handler, until the loop finishes on its own. Also debounce or disable the export trigger while one is in flight, a second click starting a second concurrent export is a real failure mode users hit exactly when they're frustrated that the first one seems stuck.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      For truly enormous exports (millions of rows), avoid building one final giant string even inside the worker, the structured-clone copy back to the main thread and the eventual <code>Blob</code> construction both still pay for a single huge in-memory buffer. Streaming the CSV out in chunks, via the File System Access API's <code>createWritable()</code> stream where available, or a chunked <code>ReadableStream</code> fed to a service-worker-backed download, avoids ever holding the complete export in memory twice (once assembled in the worker, once again as the Blob), which matters once row counts move from hundreds of thousands into the millions.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

